WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.335
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:03.636
We're here with
Bruce Springsteen.

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:06.005
So you know I've got three kids.
They're all really young.

00:00:06.005 --> 00:00:07.673
They're under 7,
and I'm trying to

00:00:07.673 --> 00:00:09.976
impress my music taste on them.

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:11.744
And I'm wondering,
your kids are older.

00:00:11.744 --> 00:00:14.614
Do they like
Bruce Springsteen music?

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:16.249
-Um...

00:00:16.249 --> 00:00:18.718
At first, they didn't know
any Bruce Springsteen music

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:21.087
for a very, very, very
long time.

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:23.089
-How do you keep it from them?

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:25.558
-Strangers would come up to us

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:27.626
and I used to always have
this thing I'd tell them.

00:00:27.626 --> 00:00:32.799
I'd say, "Look, my job?
I'm Barney for adults, alright?

00:00:32.799 --> 00:00:37.837
That's why people
keep coming up to us."

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:40.440
And then eventually,
one of my children came home

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.575
from kindergarten and said,

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:48.448
"Dad, what is
'Tenth Avenue Freeze-Out'?"

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:52.351
And I said, "Well, it's
a song that dad wrote."

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:53.752
"Play it for me."

00:00:53.752 --> 00:00:55.955
I got my guitar,
and I started --

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.089
♪ Tenth Avenue... ♪

00:00:57.089 --> 00:00:58.691
"Play it for real."

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:00.393
[ Laughter ]

00:01:00.393 --> 00:01:01.861
Okay. [ Chuckles ]

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:05.731
And I played him the song
and he got it.

00:01:05.731 --> 00:01:09.502
And then totally ignored
that part of my life for

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:11.738
most of the rest of my life,
to this day.

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:14.573
They get
a kick out of it sometimes.

00:01:14.573 --> 00:01:18.777
But for the most part...

00:01:18.777 --> 00:01:22.047
I always say, no child

00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:26.152
wants to see 50,000 people
cheer their parents.

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:27.953
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.953 --> 00:01:33.526
You may want to see
50,000 people boo your parents.

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:35.127
That would be fun.

00:01:35.127 --> 00:01:37.062
That would be interesting.

00:01:37.062 --> 00:01:40.132
But who wants to see 50,000
people cheer their parents?

00:01:40.132 --> 00:01:41.801
No kid does. No kid.

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:45.538
So we have a very
peaceful coexistence

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.607
where they pretend
it never happened.

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.777
[ Laughter ]

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:53.846
You know,
they picked their own heroes.

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:56.215
They don't need me.
-There you go.

00:01:56.215 --> 00:01:58.918
But they have in you,
I know you're a grandfather.

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.352
Congratulations.
-I am.

00:02:00.352 --> 00:02:03.590
-And this is you.
Is this the son who just had --

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:04.790
-Yes, he is.

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:06.225
-This is a lovely photo
of you and your son.

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:08.895
Really keeping it together
there, Bruce.

00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:11.630
I'm not saying, he doesn't
look like a weakling,

00:02:11.630 --> 00:02:15.167
but you're just arm-for-arm
with him right there.

00:02:15.167 --> 00:02:16.935
-Not too bad for 72.

00:02:16.935 --> 00:02:18.538
-It's not too bad at all.

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:21.774
[ Cheering ]

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:23.443
If they're gonna let you
pick up the baby,

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:24.910
you've got to prove
that you've got the arms for it.

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:26.145
-I know.

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:28.648
We've been so happy
with the granddaughter.

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:31.450
Sam's great.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:32.652
He's been a great dad,

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:35.554
and he's got a beautiful wife,
Alex.

00:02:35.554 --> 00:02:38.925
Love you, Sam and Alex, and...

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:40.493
Lily Harper.

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:42.194
-There you go, Lily Harper.
That's a beautiful name.

00:02:42.194 --> 00:02:46.698
-So what is Thanksgiving
at the Springsteen house like?

00:02:46.698 --> 00:02:48.167
-It's the usual thing.

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:51.437
My main thing on Thanksgiving
is not the turkey,

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:54.006
it is the sweet potatoes
covered with the marshmallows.

00:02:54.006 --> 00:02:55.541
-Oh, really?
-Yeah.

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:57.977
-I've had some people
looking down their nose recently

00:02:57.977 --> 00:02:59.312
at the marshmallows
and the sweet potato,

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:00.479
but you're holding firm on this?

00:03:00.479 --> 00:03:01.947
-Totally.
-Alright.

00:03:01.947 --> 00:03:06.653
-And canned cranberry sauce.
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:08.287
[ Cheering ]

00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:09.888
-I don't want to see

00:03:09.888 --> 00:03:13.493
any real cranberries
in that cranberry sauce.

00:03:13.493 --> 00:03:16.195
I want it coming out of the can
slice by slice,

00:03:16.195 --> 00:03:18.230
so I can put it on my chicken.
-Absolutely.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.800
I feel like it was not a thing
that was made

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:21.934
for the organic version.

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.435
-No.

00:03:23.435 --> 00:03:25.971
-It was made to exist in a can,
and anything outside of it...

00:03:25.971 --> 00:03:27.774
-It's a great day.
We have a lot of fun.

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:30.342
Usually most of the family
tries to get together,

00:03:30.342 --> 00:03:34.013
and, you know.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:36.549
I mean, it's this bizarre day.

00:03:36.549 --> 00:03:40.352
It revolves around eating,
you know?

00:03:40.352 --> 00:03:41.988
It's just kind of weird.

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:44.790
[ Laughter ]

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:46.292
But it's good.

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:49.662
-You're also part of this tour
going international.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:50.829
You're gonna be in Europe,

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:52.364
and I remember once,
years and years ago,

00:03:52.364 --> 00:03:54.566
I saw you in Holland.

00:03:54.566 --> 00:03:56.001
And I thought the most
interesting thing,

00:03:56.001 --> 00:03:58.004
and it must be fascinating
for you,

00:03:58.004 --> 00:04:00.039
is when people sing along
to your songs,

00:04:00.039 --> 00:04:01.908
which are in many cases,

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:03.876
these real
slice-of-Americana songs,

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:06.545
with thick European accents.

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:07.647
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:10.216
-I remember you doing
"Hungry Heart,"

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:11.784
and just hearing a bunch of
Dutch people being like,

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:13.385
[Imitates heavy accent]
"I got a wife and kid

00:04:13.385 --> 00:04:15.121
in Baltimore, Jack."

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:17.757
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:22.027
-Well, you know, first of all,
it's a shock to me

00:04:22.027 --> 00:04:26.199
that when I started scrubbing
away in my little bedroom

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:27.566
in Freehold,

00:04:27.566 --> 00:04:29.435
that if I could have
ever imagined

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.971
that thousands of miles away,

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:35.808
that people who do not
speak or understand English

00:04:35.808 --> 00:04:38.277
are going to sing your music?

00:04:38.277 --> 00:04:40.012
And that you're gonna go out
and you're gonna be greeted --

00:04:40.012 --> 00:04:44.650
I mean, it still completely --

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.452
It still blows my mind,
you know?

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:48.254
It's a wonderful thing.

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:50.590
We have incredible fans
in Europe,

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:52.324
where we have
our biggest audience.

00:04:52.324 --> 00:04:54.993
2/3 of our audience
is in Europe.

00:04:54.993 --> 00:04:59.265
It's much bigger than our
audience in the United States.

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:01.734
And it's just...

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:07.539
They're a deeply involved
and committed audience

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:10.910
that is always there,
year after year.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:12.244
It's pretty amazing.

00:05:12.244 --> 00:05:13.879
-Well, I think they're gonna
be pretty happy

00:05:13.879 --> 00:05:15.080
that you're coming back,

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.548
and we are just thrilled
to have you with us.

00:05:16.548 --> 00:05:18.016
Thanks so much
for making the time.

00:05:18.016 --> 00:05:20.253
Really appreciate it.

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:21.988
That's Bruce Springsteen,
everybody.

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:24.790
His 21st album,
"Only the Strong Survive,"

00:05:24.790 --> 00:05:26.091
is available now.

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:28.394
Stick around. We'll be right
back with Mike Birbiglia.

